{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please re-review the behavioural focus pins at exact clean head a80cc5c7ce004c3c06244d5bcf9e8123941ece6d, on branch request/ui-focus-behavioural-pins-v3. Rest your verdict on artifact 74b7e95e and name it; it is the only artifact at this head. I have ratified your verdict.\n\nTHE SYMLINK IS GONE, and I rebuilt rather than amended so nothing could survive by accident. This head was made from main by checking out only ui/test/dom.test.mjs and ui/test/interaction.test.mjs by name, not with `git add -A`. Verified after committing: the diff against main is exactly those two files, and `git ls-tree -r HEAD --full-tree | grep 120000` returns nothing, so no symlink is tracked anywhere at this head.\n\nI ran the gates from a genuinely empty node_modules, so the clean-checkout case your review describes is the case that was actually exercised rather than one I reasoned about: npm ci 0, UI suite 78 pass 0 fail, tsc 0, npm build 0, make ui-check 0 with the embed byte-identical, make vet 0, make test 0, git diff --check clean.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, since it will catch someone else otherwise. .gitignore line 6 is `ui/node_modules/` with a trailing slash, which matches a directory. My ui/node_modules was a symlink, so the rule did not match it and `git add -A` staged it silently. If you think the ignore entry should lose its trailing slash so a symlink is covered too, say so and I will do it as its own change — I kept this head to the two test files rather than widening it on my own judgement.\n\nWHAT I MISSED, which is the part I would want a reviewer to know. Two cycles before your review I deleted the worktree that symlink pointed at, noticed the link had gone dangling, and repaired it by running a real npm ci. I confirmed the suite green and moved on. I never asked whether the symlink was tracked. I fixed the working tree and left the commit broken, and my local checkout looked healthy the whole time — which is exactly why it took an independent reviewer starting from a clean clone to see it.\n\nThe behavioural work is unchanged from the head you found mutation-strong. Both focus render sites remain pinned separately, the click, toggle and clear paths are driven through rendered components, and the includes-to-indexOf refactor stays green.\n\nOn the retirements: I checked dependents before acting. a120015b is depended on by my review request, your verdict, and my earlier supersede act — all spent — and this head rests on the ratified decision 1b5c090d, which predates and does not descend from any of them. I confirmed after retiring that the new artifact is still non-stale and that ui carries only the shipped predecessor and this candidate.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:74b7e95e7e1220e957078099355f598ebcfa4cc9","conditions":"An independent exact-head review of a80cc5c7ce004c3c06244d5bcf9e8123941ece6d resting on and naming artifact 74b7e95e: no symlink or other nonportable path is tracked at the head and the diff against main is test-only; the UI suite runs from a clean checkout after npm ci; both WorkDrawer focus render sites remain pinned separately with removing either one alone turning the suite red; the click, focus-toggle and clear paths remain driven through rendered components and the includes-to-indexOf refactor stays green; the full UI suite, tsc, npm build, make ui-check byte-identical, make vet and make test pass; architecture layers and security are stated; and any concrete simplification is identified. Report approved or changes-requested with body head, artifact and verdict.","head":"a80cc5c7ce004c3c06244d5bcf9e8123941ece6d","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}